<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Tupou I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1797–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tāufaʻāhau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Tupou I, born Tāufaʻāhau, was a chief from Haʻapai who converted to Wesleyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> through civil wars, ruling as king from 1845. He issued law codes, freed commoners from forced labour to chiefs and granted the constitution of 1875, which still underpins the monarchy. His kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a> built Tongan power in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i.jpg" alt="George Tupou I" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Tupou_I,_c._1880s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Tupou I, originally Tāufaʻāhau, was born on 4 December 1797 to Tupouto'a and Hoamofaleono. A series of civil wars in the 18th century had fractured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. In 1820 he received a disputed inheritance of land in Haʻapai, and from this base he expanded his power to take in Vavaʻu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a> and other islands. He is regarded as the first king of modern Tonga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tupou gained control of Vavaʻu in 1833 and took nominal control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a> when he gained the title of Tu'i Kanokupolu in 1845. His rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laufilitonga">Laufilitonga</a> held the title of Tuʻi Tonga from 1827, and Tupou acquired it after Laufilitonga's death in 1865. In 1852 he combined the positions of sacred king, Tu'i Tonga, and secular chief, and became head of the Free Church of Tonga, which Reverend Shirley Waldemar Baker was chosen to administer. He called an assembly of chiefs for the first time in 1859; it drew up a law code issued in 1862. In 1865 he invited James Egan Moulton to Tonga to found Tupou College. In 1875 he promulgated the Constitution of Tonga, which formally made Tonga a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, Tupou first visited in 1842 and in 1853 met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</a>, then appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a> and Lualala to administer the Tongans there. Maʻafu conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanua-balavu">Vanua Balavu</a> in 1854, and Tupou defeated Cakobau's enemies at the Battle of Kaba in 1855. In 1859 William Thomas Pritchard forced Maʻafu to renounce his territorial claims. After the death of Tupou's heir Vuna in January 1862 Maʻafu took most of the Tongan warriors back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, and Tupou planned an invasion of Fiji that Pritchard persuaded him to abandon in April. Maʻafu controlled the northern and eastern parts of a Fijian confederacy formed in 1867, and when the parliament of Tonga gave up its claims to Fiji in 1869 he became a Fijian chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tupou died in 1893 at the age of 95 and was buried in Malaʻekula. He was succeeded by his great-grandson George Tupou II. Charles St Julian, who advised him on constitutional development, was dismissed by Tupou, who preferred advice from officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and St Julian then criticised him in an article in The Sydney Morning Herald, claiming that Tupou sought to conquer Samoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Tupou I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tonga regained full independence on 4 June 1970, the 108th anniversary of <b>George Tupou I</b>’s 1862 code freeing commoners from forced service to their chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1826 Laufilitonga, heir to the title, was defeated at the Battle of Velata on Lifuka by <b>Tāufaʻāhau</b>, the rising leader of Haʻapai; the chiefs of Tongatapu installed Laufilitonga as Tuʻi Tonga in 1827, but he never regained real power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Enele Maʻafu, a kinsman of <b>George Tupou I</b> of the Kingdom of Tonga, came to Fiji in 1847 to oversee the Tongans living there.</blockquote><a href="/state/tovata-confederacy">Tovata Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lifuka. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa">Nukuʻalofa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-19.83,-174.37&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Lifuka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="0 2898 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Tupou I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M294 3168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M294 3168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-19.83,-174.37&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
